// Binary:  N = b_n*2^n  + ... + b_0*2^0`},{question:"Why do computer scientists and hardware engineers use Hexadecimal (Base-16) instead of writing raw Binary?",shortAnswer:"Hexadecimal provides a compact human-readable representation where exactly 1 hex digit represents 4 binary bits (1 nibble).",explanation:"A 32-bit or 64-bit binary memory address (e.g. 11111111000010101100010111100010) is unwieldy and error-prone to read. Because $16 = 2^4$, each hexadecimal character represents exactly 4 binary bits (e.g. 0xFF0AC5E2), making byte-level inspection straightforward.",hint:"4 bits = 1 nibble = 1 hex digit (0-9, A-F).",level:"basic",codeExample:`// In C/C++:
